      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks that makes sense. i think i've heard people call it \"spooky\" before though. why is that? what is it about the connection that makes it seem so weird or \"spooky\" compared to normal physics?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"spooky\" label comes from Albert Einstein's famous quote, \"Spukhafte Fernwirkung\" or \"spooky action at a distance.\" He was referring to the fact that entangled particles can affect each other instantaneously, regardless of the distance between them. This seems to defy the fundamental principles of space and time, which are based on the speed of light.\n\nThink of it like a game of hide-and-seek with a group of very, very fast and sneaky little creatures, like a group of goblins. They can hide in different parts of the forest, but they can still communicate with each other instantly, no matter how far apart they are. It's like they have a secret network of underground tunnels that allow them to move information faster than the speed of light.\n\nEinstein was uncomfortable with this idea, as it seemed to imply that the universe was connected in a way that was not yet understood. He believed that information couldn't travel faster than light, and that entanglement was a kind of \"magic\" that didn't fit with the laws of physics as we knew them.\n\nBut, as it turns out, entanglement is a real phenomenon that has been experimentally confirmed many times. It",
